<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferrara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferrara is a city of Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, near the Po. From the 13th century it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a>, whose court made it a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> art, music and letters, home to the poets Ariosto and Tasso. When the main Este line died out in 1597 the Papal States took the city, and its planned Renaissance extension, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea">Addizione Erculea</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferrara.jpg" alt="Ferrara" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nbisi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castello_dall%27alto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ferrara lies on the Po di Volano, a branch channel of the Po, about 44 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>. The nearness of the Po has been a constant concern, with recurrent disastrous floods, the latest in 1951. The first documented settlements in the province date from the 6th century BCE. The Etruscan town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spina">Spina</a>, near the ancient mouth of the Po, was lost until modern times, when drainage works in 1922 revealed a necropolis of over 4,000 tombs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ferrara first appears in a document of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> of 753, who captured it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> gave it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in 754 or 756. In 988 the Church ceded it to the House of Canossa, and at the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Tuscany</a> in 1115 it became a free commune. In the 12th century the Este backed the Ghibelline Salinguerra against the Guelph Adelardi and in the end reaped the benefit. In 1264 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obizzo-ii-deste">Obizzo II d'Este</a> was proclaimed lifelong ruler, ending the communal period.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borso-deste">Borso d'Este</a> was made duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Reggio in 1452 by Emperor Frederick III and duke of Ferrara in 1471 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a>. Lionello and above all Ercole I were among the most important patrons of the arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in their time. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea">Addizione Erculea</a>, planned by Biagio Rossetti for Ercole I, is regarded as one of the most important examples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> urban planning.</p><p>A war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, fought and lost in 1482 to 1484, struck the city hard. Alfonso I, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucrezia-borgia">Lucrezia Borgia</a>, overcame the papal and Spanish armies at the Battle of Ravenna in 1512 with artillery from his own foundry. An earthquake in 1570 caused the economy to collapse.</p><p>When Alfonso II died without heirs in 1597, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a> lost Ferrara to the Papal States, under which it remained for almost 300 years, a period of steady decline; in 1792 its population was only 27,000. It was briefly part of the Napoleonic Kingdom of Italy from 1805 to 1814, and was restored to the pope in 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ferrara is a city of Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and capital of its province. Its Este Castle, begun in 1385 with four bastions and a moat, stands in the centre, and the Cathedral of Saint George was consecrated in 1135. About 9 kilometres of brick walls still encircle the town. Its Jewish community is the only one in Emilia-Romagna with a continuous presence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to the present. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has designated the city a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferrara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…annexed Avignon and the Comtat Venaissin in 1791, Napoleon forced Pius VI to cede the Legations of Bologna, <b>Ferrara</b> and Romagna at the Treaty of Tolentino in 1797, and in 1798 French troops proclaimed a Roman Republic and carried the pope into exile, where he died in 1799.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…14th to the 16th century a spiritual revival grew from the preaching friars, lay movements such as the devotio moderna, printing and Christian humanism, and councils with reforming agendas met at Constance in 1415, at Basel, <b>Ferrara</b> and Florence, and at the Fifth Council of the Lateran (1512–1517).</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The council that met at <b>Ferrara</b> and then Florence in 1438–1439 to reunite the Greek and Latin churches brought Byzantine scholars to Italy: Gemistos Plethon lectured in Florence on Plato, and Bessarion, made a cardinal of the Roman Church in 1439, settled in Italy and later gave his…</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>With the mediation of the Pope, Venice, Milan, Florence and the Gonzaga signed a peace at <b>Ferrara</b>, ending the second campaign of the Wars in Lombardy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Eugene IV presided over the first solemn session of the council he had moved from Basel to <b>Ferrara</b>, attended by the Eastern Roman Emperor and the Patriarch of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The members of the council who had stayed at Basel after Eugene IV moved it to <b>Ferrara</b> affirmed its ecumenical character and suspended the pope.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
